{"name":"Stormy Sea","hex":"#6e8082","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"stormy","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of or pertaining to storms.","Characterized by, or proceeding from, a storm; subject to storms; agitated with strong winds and heavy rain."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/stormy"},{"term":"sea","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Semai."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A large body of salt water.","The ocean; the continuous body of salt water covering a majority of the Earth's surface."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sea"}],"entries":[{"title":"Stormy Seas","blocks":[{"text":"Stormy Seas is a 1923 American silent drama film directed by J.P. McGowan and starring McGowan, Helen Holmes, and Leslie Casey."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/Stormy_Seas_%281923_film%29.jpg/330px-Stormy_Seas_%281923_film%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stormy_Seas"},{"title":"Flip the Frog","blocks":[{"text":"Flip the Frog is an animated cartoon character created by American animator Ub Iwerks. He starred in a series of 38 cartoons produced by Celebrity Pictures and distributed by Metro-Goldwyn-Mayer from 1930 to 1933. The series had many recurring characters, including Flip's dog, a mule named Orace, and a dizzy neighborhood spinster."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/59/Flip_the_Frog_poster.jpg/330px-Flip_the_Frog_poster.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flip_the_Frog"},{"title":"Stormy Sea (Nolde)","blocks":[{"text":"Stormy Sea is a watercolor landscape painting by German painter Emil Nolde, from 1930. The painting is held in the collection of the Sprengel Museum, in Hanover."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stormy_Sea_(Nolde)"},{"title":"Sea","blocks":[{"text":"A sea is a very large body of salty water. The term has two distinct meanings. The first is the interconnected body of seawater that spans most of Earth, known as the sea or the ocean. The second is a named body of water: usually either a marginal sea, or a large, nearly landlocked body of water."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Mediterranean_Sea_16.61811E_38.99124N.jpg/330px-Mediterranean_Sea_16.61811E_38.99124N.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sea"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/Fiddlesticks.jpg/330px-Fiddlesticks.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/Fiddlesticks.jpg/330px-Fiddlesticks.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Flip the Frog in his first appearance, Fiddlesticks, opposite an unnamed mouse, who bears a striking resemblance to Mickey Mouse","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFiddlesticks.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Flip_the_Frog_Model_Sheet.jpg/330px-Flip_the_Frog_Model_Sheet.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Flip_the_Frog_Model_Sheet.jpg/330px-Flip_the_Frog_Model_Sheet.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Model sheet of Flip the Frog, c. 1931, showing his evolution to less frog-like appearance","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFlip_the_Frog_Model_Sheet.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/BlueMarble-2001-2002.jpg/500px-BlueMarble-2001-2002.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thu